# Line-ending policy — deliberately narrow.
#
# The team develops on Windows and deploys to Linux containers. A shell script that reaches a
# container with CRLF endings fails in a way that reads as a missing file:
#
#     exec /usr/local/bin/docker-entrypoint.sh: no such file or directory
#
# …because the kernel takes the trailing `\r` as part of the interpreter path on the `#!/bin/sh`
# line. The same class of breakage hits nginx configs and anything else parsed inside a container.
#
# Only files that Linux executes or parses are pinned. There is deliberately **no** blanket
# `* text=auto`: that would mark the entire tree as text and rewrite every .py and .ts file to
# CRLF on the next Windows checkout, producing a repository-wide diff that has nothing to do with
# deployment. Source line endings stay exactly as they are today.

*.sh                text eol=lf
*.conf              text eol=lf
Dockerfile          text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile        text eol=lf
.dockerignore       text eol=lf
docker-compose*.yml text eol=lf
